ENTRIES = [
("Foundations",
 "What Digital Preservation Is: Keeping Bits Meaningful",
 "Digital preservation is the discipline of keeping digital content usable across decades: not just storing bits, but ensuring those bits remain readable, understandable, and trustworthy as media decay, formats die, and institutions change. It differs from backup - backup restores last Tuesday's files after a crash, while preservation guarantees that a dataset remains authentic and renderable in 2076. Preservation works on three fronts simultaneously: the bits themselves (fixity, redundancy), the formats (migration, emulation), and the meaning (metadata, documentation). An archive that stores files without addressing all three is a hard drive with ambitions, not a preservation system.",
 ["Preservation keeps content usable for decades; backup restores recent files after crashes",
  "Three fronts: bits (fixity, redundancy), formats (migration, emulation), meaning (metadata)",
  "Authenticity - proving content is unchanged - is as important as availability",
  "Preservation is planned for institutional lifetimes, not hardware lifetimes",
  "Storing files without a preservation plan is not preservation"],
 "The distinction between backup and archiving and a written preservation intent stating what is kept, for whom, for how long.",
 "ap_checksum"),

("Foundations",
 "Bit Rot and Media Decay: The Slow Enemies",
 "Storage media decay: magnetic domains weaken, optical dyes fade, flash cells leak charge, and cosmic rays flip bits. Bit rot is the silent corruption of stored data - a single flipped bit in a text file is invisible, while one in a compressed archive can destroy the whole file. Studies of large disk populations show unrecoverable read errors are a matter of when, not if. The defense is not better media (all media decay) but process: redundant copies on diverse media, regular fixity verification against checksums, and repair from good copies. Preservation assumes every storage device is already failing and designs accordingly.",
 ["All media decay: magnetic, optical, and flash all have failure modes",
  "One flipped bit can destroy a compressed archive while hiding in text",
  "Unrecoverable read errors are when, not if",
  "Defense is process - redundancy, fixity checks, repair - not better media",
  "Assume every device is already failing and design for it"],
 "Redundant storage on at least two media types and scheduled checksum verification.",
 "ap_lockss"),

("Foundations",
 "Checksums and Fixity: Proving Nothing Changed",
 "A checksum is a short fingerprint computed from a file's bytes: SHA-256 produces a 64-character fingerprint so sensitive that changing one bit changes about half the characters. Fixity is the property of being unchanged, and checksums are how archives prove it - compute at ingest, store in a manifest, recompute on schedule, and any mismatch means corruption or tampering. MD5 and SHA-1 are retired for security purposes (collision attacks exist), but SHA-256 remains the preservation workhorse. The checksum does not prevent damage; it guarantees damage cannot hide. An archive that cannot prove its files are unchanged cannot claim to preserve them.",
 ["SHA-256 fingerprints are bit-sensitive: one flipped bit changes the fingerprint",
  "Fixity = proven unchanged; checksums are the proof mechanism",
  "Compute at ingest, store, recompute on schedule, investigate mismatches",
  "SHA-256 is the current workhorse; MD5/SHA-1 are retired for security",
  "Checksums don't prevent damage - they guarantee damage cannot hide"],
 "SHA-256 tooling and a manifest recording one checksum per file at ingest.",
 "ap_checksum"),

("Foundations",
 "The Checksum Manifest: SHA-256 Inventories",
 "A checksum manifest is the archive's inventory with integrity built in: one line per file, each pairing the file's path with its SHA-256 fingerprint, often with file sizes and timestamps. Manifests travel with the content they describe and are themselves protected - a manifest stored only alongside the files it guards dies with them, so copies live separately. Verification is mechanical: recompute every fingerprint, compare to the manifest, report mismatches. BagIt, the Library of Congress packaging format, standardizes manifests as tag files inside a 'bag' directory. The manifest is the document that turns a pile of files into an accountable collection.",
 ["One line per file: path plus SHA-256 fingerprint, with sizes and dates",
  "Manifests travel with content but are also stored separately",
  "Verification is mechanical: recompute, compare, report",
  "BagIt standardizes manifests as tag files in a bag directory",
  "The manifest turns a pile of files into an accountable collection"],
 "A manifest generator and verifier plus separate secure storage for manifest copies.",
 "ap_checksum"),

("Foundations",
 "File Formats: Open Beats Proprietary",
 "A file format is a deal between today's software and tomorrow's readers, and open formats keep the deal honest: their specifications are published, multiple independent programs can read them, and no single vendor's survival decides the format's fate. Plain text (UTF-8), CSV, PDF/A (the archival PDF profile), TIFF, and PNG are preservation-friendly; proprietary formats with secret specifications are preservation risks - when the vendor drops support, the content becomes hostage. The preservation rule is blunt: prefer open, documented, widely-supported formats for archival copies, and keep the original too when conversion might lose something. Format choice is a fifty-year decision made today.",
 ["Open formats: published specs, multiple readers, no vendor hostage situations",
  "Preservation-friendly: UTF-8 text, CSV, PDF/A, TIFF, PNG",
  "Proprietary secret-spec formats are preservation risks",
  "Keep archival copies in open formats; keep originals when conversion may lose data",
  "Format choice is a fifty-year decision"],
 "A list of accepted archival formats and conversion tools from proprietary originals.",
 "ap_pronom"),

("Foundations",
 "Format Registries: PRONOM and Identification",
 "You cannot preserve what you cannot identify. Format registries like PRONOM (maintained by The UK National Archives) catalog thousands of file formats with their identifying signatures - the magic numbers and byte patterns at a file's start - plus their risk status and relationships. Identification tools (such as DROID, built on PRONOM signatures) scan collections and report what each file actually is, which routinely surprises: the '.doc' that is really a ZIP, the 'TIFF' that is really a proprietary variant. Identification is the census before the preservation plan: the archive must know its format population before it can assess risk or plan migrations.",
 ["PRONOM catalogs formats with identifying byte signatures and risk status",
  "Identification tools scan collections and report what files actually are",
  "Extensions lie; magic numbers tell the truth",
  "Run identification before planning: census first, strategy second",
  "Unknown formats are unmanageable risks until identified"],
 "PRONOM-based identification tooling run across the full collection.",
 "ap_pronom"),

("Foundations",
 "The OAIS Model: ISO 14721 Reference",
 "OAIS (Open Archival Information System, ISO 14721) is the reference model the whole preservation field shares: it defines the archive's job, its information packages, and its functional parts in vendor-neutral language. The model names the players - Producer (who submits), Management (who sets policy), Consumer (who uses) - and the functions - Ingest, Archival Storage, Data Management, Administration, Preservation Planning, Access. OAIS does not tell you which software to buy; it tells you what functions any trustworthy archive must perform, giving auditors, funders, and successors a common vocabulary. An archive that can describe itself in OAIS terms can be evaluated; one that cannot is a black box.",
 ["ISO 14721 defines the archive's functions in vendor-neutral language",
  "Players: Producer, Management, Consumer; functions: Ingest through Access",
  "OAIS describes what must be done, not which software to buy",
  "Common vocabulary lets auditors and successors evaluate the archive",
  "Describe your archive in OAIS terms or remain a black box"],
 "The OAIS standard text and a mapping of local workflows to its six functions.",
 "ap_oais"),

("Foundations",
 "SIPs, AIPs, DIPs: Packages Through the Lifecycle",
 "OAIS tracks content through three package types. The SIP (Submission Information Package) is what the producer delivers - content plus whatever metadata came with it, quality uncontrolled. Ingest transforms it into the AIP (Archival Information Package): content plus full preservation metadata, fixity information, and representation details, built for the long haul and never casually altered. When a consumer requests content, the archive derives a DIP (Dissemination Information Package) - the AIP rendered into a usable form, perhaps converted to a current format. The AIP is the preservation master; SIPs and DIPs come and go, but the AIP endures, versioned and fixity-checked, for decades.",
 ["SIP: what the producer submits, quality uncontrolled",
  "AIP: the preservation master - content plus full metadata, built for decades",
  "DIP: the usable copy derived for each consumer request",
  "Ingest transforms SIP to AIP; Access derives DIPs from AIPs",
  "Never casually alter an AIP; changes become new versioned AIPs"],
 "An ingest workflow producing versioned AIPs and a dissemination path deriving DIPs.",
 "ap_oais"),

("Foundations",
 "Redundancy: Multiple Copies, Multiple Places",
 "One copy is no copies. Redundancy is preservation's oldest rule: keep multiple copies of every AIP, on different storage media, in different buildings, preferably under different administrations and power grids. Geographic separation defeats local disasters - fire, flood, and backhoe all respect distance. Media diversity defeats correlated failure - a bad firmware batch cannot kill tape and disk alike. The copies are verified against each other and against the manifests; a copy that disagrees is repaired from the quorum. Redundancy is not paranoia; it is the arithmetic of decades, where rare events become certainties.",
 ["Multiple copies on diverse media in geographically separated locations",
  "Separation defeats local disaster; diversity defeats correlated failure",
  "Copies verified against manifests and against each other",
  "Disagreeing copies are repaired from the quorum",
  "Over decades, rare events become certainties - redundancy is arithmetic"],
 "At least three copies on two media types in two locations, with cross-verification.",
 "ap_lockss"),

("Foundations",
 "Backup vs Archiving: Last Tuesday vs 2076",
 "Backup and archiving solve different problems and confusing them loses data. Backup captures recent state for rapid restore after failure - it is short-horizon, overwritten on rotation, and cares about recovery speed. Archiving preserves selected content for the long term - it is long-horizon, immutable by design, and cares about authenticity and renderability decades out. Backups get rotated and eventually overwritten; archives get fixity-checked and migrated. An organization needs both: backups for operational continuity, archives for institutional memory. The test is simple: ask what happens to each system in thirty years. The backup is long gone; the archive is just getting started.",
 ["Backup: short-horizon, rotated, optimized for fast restore",
  "Archive: long-horizon, immutable, optimized for authenticity over decades",
  "Backups get overwritten; archives get fixity-checked and migrated",
  "You need both: backups for operations, archives for memory",
  "The thirty-year test separates the two instantly"],
 "Separate backup and archive systems with different retention, verification, and media policies.",
 "ap_schedule"),

("Foundations",
 "Metadata for Preservation: PREMIS Basics",
 "Content without metadata is a box of unlabeled photographs. Preservation metadata records what the content is, where it came from, what has been done to it, and what is allowed to be done - the PREMIS Data Dictionary (from the Library of Congress) standardizes this into four entities: Objects (the files), Events (things done to them), Agents (who or what did it), and Rights (what is permitted). Every fixity check, migration, and access becomes an Event linked to its Object and Agent. Without this record, a future archivist inherits files but no history - and history is what makes the files trustworthy. Capture preservation metadata at the moment of action, never reconstructed later.",
 ["PREMIS: Objects, Events, Agents, Rights - the four entities of preservation metadata",
  "Every action (fixity check, migration) becomes a linked Event",
  "Rights record what preservation actions are permitted",
  "Capture metadata at the moment of action, never reconstructed later",
  "Files without history are untrustworthy; metadata is the history"],
 "PREMIS-aware tooling capturing events automatically at each preservation action.",
 "ap_premis"),

("Foundations",
 "The Designated Community: Preserving For Whom",
 "OAIS insists every archive name its Designated Community - the group the archive preserves content FOR - because 'usable' depends on the user. A scientific data archive serves researchers who understand the instruments; a public records archive serves citizens who need plain-language finding aids. The community's knowledge base decides how much representation information the archive must keep: experts need less explanation, the general public needs more. Communities change over decades, so archives monitor theirs and adjust documentation before the knowledge gap becomes unbridgeable. Preservation without a defined community is storage hoping for luck.",
 ["The Designated Community is who the archive preserves content for",
  "'Usable' is defined by the community's knowledge, not the archivist's",
  "Keep representation information matched to what the community understands",
  "Monitor the community over decades; adjust before knowledge gaps open",
  "No defined community means storage hoping for luck"],
 "A written designated-community definition reviewed on a fixed schedule.",
 "ap_oais"),

("Compounded",
 "Format Obsolescence: How Formats Die",
 "Formats die slowly, then suddenly: first the vendor stops updating the reader, then new operating systems break it, then the last expert retires, and finally the files become unreadable bit-strings. Warning signs are standard - declining market share, proprietary lock-in, reader software that only runs on obsolete operating systems, formats whose specifications were never published. Obsolescence is not about age; some forty-year-old plain-text files open perfectly while ten-year-old proprietary formats are already stranded. Archives watch their format population the way doctors watch vital signs, because catching obsolescence early means migrating at leisure instead of in panic.",
 ["Death pattern: vendor abandonment, OS breakage, expert retirement, unreadability",
  "Warning signs: declining share, lock-in, obsolete-only readers, secret specs",
  "Age doesn't kill formats; secrecy and abandonment do",
  "Watch format vital signs continuously, not at crisis time",
  "Early detection means migration at leisure; late means panic"],
 "Continuous format-population monitoring with defined obsolescence warning thresholds.",
 "ap_risk"),

("Compounded",
 "Obsolescence Risk Scoring",
 "Not every format needs the same urgency, so archives score risk: openness of the specification, number of independent readers, size of the user community, availability of the spec, and dependence on single vendors each contribute to a score. High-risk formats get migration plans now; low-risk formats get watched. Scoring is repeated on schedule because risk moves - a healthy format can sicken when its vendor is acquired. The scores drive the preservation budget: money follows risk, not sentiment. A risk register, reviewed yearly, turns vague format anxiety into a prioritized work plan with owners and deadlines.",
 ["Score openness, reader count, community size, spec availability, vendor dependence",
  "High risk gets migration plans now; low risk gets monitoring",
  "Re-score on schedule - vendor acquisitions can sicken healthy formats overnight",
  "Budgets follow risk scores, not sentiment or loudest advocate",
  "A yearly-reviewed risk register turns anxiety into a work plan"],
 "A defined scoring rubric, a format risk register, and yearly re-scoring with owners assigned.",
 "ap_risk"),

("Compounded",
 "Migration: Moving Content to New Formats",
 "Migration is preservation's workhorse: converting content from an endangered format to a safer one while preserving the significant properties - the characteristics that must survive, like text content, structure, and image resolution. The migration plan names the source and target formats, the conversion tool and version, the significant properties, and the validation method. Validation is the soul of migration: checksums of content layers, page counts, spot-checked rendering, and quarantined failures with logs. Every migration is recorded as a PREMIS Event, and the original is retained - migration adds a new representation, it never destroys the old one. Migrate at leisure, validate ruthlessly.",
 ["Convert endangered formats to safer ones, preserving significant properties",
  "Plan states source, target, tool version, properties, validation method",
  "Validate ruthlessly: content checksums, counts, spot-checked rendering",
  "Record every migration as a PREMIS Event; retain the original",
  "Migration adds representations; it never destroys originals"],
 "Tested conversion tooling, defined significant properties per format, and validation procedures.",
 "ap_migrate"),

("Compounded",
 "Emulation: Running Old Software Forever",
 "Some content cannot be migrated without losing its essence - interactive art, complex databases, software itself. Emulation preserves the original bits unchanged and instead preserves the environment: emulators recreate obsolete hardware and operating systems on modern machines, so the original software runs as it did. The archive then preserves three things: the content, the original software, and the emulator - plus documentation of the whole stack. Emulation's cost is complexity and its risk is emulator rot (emulators themselves need preserving), but for behavior-dependent content it is the only faithful strategy. Choose emulation when the experience IS the content.",
 ["Preserve original bits unchanged; recreate the obsolete environment instead",
  "Archive content plus original software plus emulator plus stack documentation",
  "Emulator rot is the meta-risk: emulators need preserving too",
  "Choose emulation when behavior and experience are the content",
  "Migration preserves information; emulation preserves experience"],
 "Working emulators for target platforms and documentation of the full emulation stack.",
 "ap_migrate"),

("Compounded",
 "Normalization: One Archival Format Per Type",
 "Normalization simplifies preservation by converting incoming content to one standard archival format per type at ingest: text to PDF/A or UTF-8, images to TIFF, audio to BWF. Fewer formats mean fewer migration paths to maintain, fewer tools to preserve, and simpler risk monitoring - the archive's format population stays small and healthy by policy. The trade-off is fidelity: normalization may discard features the original format supported, so significant properties must be defined and the original retained alongside the normalized copy. Normalization is a policy decision documented in the preservation plan, not a casual conversion - it shapes the archive's workload for decades.",
 ["One standard archival format per content type, applied at ingest",
  "Fewer formats mean fewer migrations, fewer tools, simpler monitoring",
  "Define significant properties; retain originals alongside normalized copies",
  "Normalization is archive policy, documented in the preservation plan",
  "It shapes the archive's workload for decades - decide deliberately"],
 "A normalization policy naming the archival format per type and significant-property definitions.",
 "ap_migrate"),

("Compounded",
 "Fixity Checking Schedules",
 "Checksums only protect if someone recomputes them, so archives schedule fixity checks by value tier: critical collections verified monthly, standard collections yearly, cold storage on a multi-year cycle - with every check logged as a PREMIS Event. Scheduling balances assurance against cost: checking everything daily wastes compute and wears media, while never checking invites silent rot. Failed checks trigger the repair workflow immediately: quarantine the suspect copy, verify against manifests, restore from a good replica, and record the whole incident. The schedule is published, the logs are kept, and auditors ask to see both.",
 ["Tier by value: critical monthly, standard yearly, cold on multi-year cycles",
  "Balance assurance against compute cost and media wear",
  "Failures trigger immediate repair: quarantine, verify, restore, record",
  "Log every check as a PREMIS Event; publish the schedule",
  "Auditors will ask to see the schedule and the logs"],
 "A tiered fixity schedule, automated checking, and a defined repair workflow.",
 "ap_schedule"),

("Compounded",
 "Repair from Redundant Copies",
 "Detection without repair is just bad news delivered efficiently. When a fixity check fails, the repair workflow activates: the suspect copy is quarantined (never trusted again until proven), the manifests identify what the good bytes should be, and a healthy replica supplies replacement bytes - verified chunk by chunk against the manifest before the copy returns to service. If no healthy replica exists, the archive escalates: forensic recovery attempts, then honest reporting of loss. Every repair is a PREMIS Event with before/after checksums. The quorum rule is absolute: never repair from a copy whose own fixity is unverified.",
 ["Quarantine suspect copies; never trust them until re-verified",
  "Replace bytes from healthy replicas, verified chunk by chunk against manifests",
  "No healthy replica means forensic recovery, then honest loss reporting",
  "Record every repair as a PREMIS Event with before/after checksums",
  "Never repair from an unverified copy - the quorum rule"],
 "Quarantine procedures, manifest-driven repair tooling, and escalation paths for total loss.",
 "ap_lockss"),

("Compounded",
 "LOCKSS: Lots of Copies Keep Stuff Safe",
 "LOCKSS (Lots of Copies Keep Stuff Safe, from Stanford) turns redundancy into a protocol: many independent archives each hold copies and continuously audit each other, voting on content integrity. When a participant's copy disagrees with the quorum, it repairs from the majority - no central authority needed, no single point of failure. The polling protocol is deliberately slow and random to resist attacks. LOCKSS networks have preserved scholarly journals through publisher bankruptcies and platform shutdowns. The lesson generalizes: cooperative, quorum-based preservation survives institutional failures that kill any single archive, however well funded.",
 ["Many independent archives hold copies and audit each other continuously",
  "Quorum voting detects damage; the majority repairs the minority",
  "No central authority, no single point of failure",
  "Slow random polling resists manipulation",
  "Cooperative preservation survives institutional deaths"],
 "Participation in a LOCKSS-style network or an equivalent quorum-audit arrangement.",
 "ap_lockss"),

("Compounded",
 "PREMIS in Depth: Objects, Events, Agents, Rights",
 "PREMIS structures preservation metadata as four linked entities. Objects are the files and bitstreams, described with fixity, format, and significant properties. Events record every preservation action - ingest, fixity check, migration, access - with date, outcome, and detail. Agents are the people, organizations, and software that perform events. Rights document the permissions that allow preservation actions (migration is copying; copying needs rights). Linking them answers any audit question: which agent ran which event on which object under which rights. Implementations serialize PREMIS as XML alongside the AIP. Depth here is what separates evidence-grade archives from file servers.",
 ["Objects: files with fixity, format, significant properties",
  "Events: every action with date, outcome, detail - the audit trail",
  "Agents: who/what acted; Rights: what actions are permitted",
  "Links answer any audit question about any object",
  "Serialize as XML with the AIP; depth makes archives evidence-grade"],
 "PREMIS XML generation at every preservation event with entity linking.",
 "ap_premis"),

("Compounded",
 "Preservation Metadata in Practice",
 "Theory becomes practice at ingest: the workflow extracts technical metadata automatically (format identification, checksums, byte counts), captures descriptive metadata from the producer, records the ingest event with its agent, and packages it all into the AIP. Automation is essential - manual metadata entry doesn't scale to millions of files - but automation needs verification: sample audits confirm the extractors are telling the truth. Metadata itself needs preservation: it is checksummed, replicated, and migrated like content, because an AIP whose metadata is lost is a box of unlabeled files. Practice means metadata pipelines, not metadata aspirations.",
 ["Extract technical metadata automatically at ingest: format, checksum, size",
  "Capture descriptive metadata from producers; record the ingest event",
  "Automate at scale, but audit samples to verify the extractors",
  "Preserve metadata like content: checksummed, replicated, migrated",
  "Metadata pipelines in production beat metadata aspirations in policy"],
 "Automated extraction tooling with sample-audit verification and metadata replication.",
 "ap_premis"),

("Compounded",
 "Disaster Planning for Archives",
 "Fire, flood, ransomware, and bankruptcy all threaten archives, and disaster planning prepares the response before the siren. The plan names an incident commander, defines collection priorities (what gets saved first), documents replica locations and access credentials, and rehearses the recovery drill on schedule - because unrehearsed plans fail. Offline and offsite copies defeat ransomware; geographic separation defeats regional disaster; tested restores defeat wishful thinking. The plan also covers the quiet disaster: funding loss, with escrowed documentation and succession agreements. Archives don't survive disasters by luck; they survive by rehearsal.",
 ["Name commanders, prioritize collections, document replica locations and credentials",
  "Rehearse recovery drills on schedule - unrehearsed plans fail",
  "Offline/offsite copies defeat ransomware; separation defeats regional disaster",
  "Tested restores beat wishful thinking every time",
  "Cover quiet disasters too: funding loss, with escrow and succession"],
 "A written disaster plan with roles, priorities, credentials, and scheduled rehearsals.",
 "ap_recovery"),

("Compounded",
 "Succession Planning: Surviving the Founders",
 "Archives outlive their founders or they fail their mission. Succession planning ensures the archive survives people: documentation of every procedure (no knowledge held only in heads), escrowed credentials and contracts, designated successor organizations for the collections, and trigger conditions that activate succession (funding thresholds, key departures). The OAIS Administration function owns this. Testing succession means asking whether a competent stranger could run the archive from the documentation alone - and fixing the gaps the answer reveals. An archive that depends on one irreplaceable person is a hobby, not an institution.",
 ["Document every procedure; no knowledge held only in heads",
  "Escrow credentials and contracts; designate successor organizations",
  "Define triggers: funding thresholds, departures that activate succession",
  "Test: could a competent stranger run this from the docs alone?",
  "Dependence on one irreplaceable person is a hobby, not an institution"],
 "Complete procedure documentation, escrowed credentials, and named successors with triggers.",
 "ap_recovery"),

("Advanced",
 "Format-Migration Plans: The Full Playbook",
 "A migration plan is preservation's battle plan, written before the first file converts. It states the trigger (risk score threshold reached), the source format population (from identification census), the target format and why it is safer, the conversion tool with exact version, the significant properties that must survive, the pilot scope, the validation method with acceptance criteria, the rollback procedure, the schedule, the staff, and the budget. It defines done: what percentage validates, how failures are quarantined, and who signs the migration report. Plans are reviewed and approved like engineering designs, because a bad migration corrupts at scale - the plan is what makes migration safe to run on a million files.",
 ["Trigger, population census, target format rationale, exact tool version",
  "Significant properties, pilot scope, validation with acceptance criteria",
  "Rollback procedure, schedule, staff, budget - the full resourcing",
  "Define done: validation rates, failure quarantine, sign-off authority",
  "Reviewed like engineering designs; bad migrations corrupt at scale"],
 "An approved migration-plan template covering triggers through sign-off, used for every migration.",
 "ap_migrate"),

("Advanced",
 "Significant Properties: What Must Survive Migration",
 "Not everything about a file matters, but the things that matter must be named: significant properties are the characteristics that must survive any preservation action - the text and structure of a document, the resolution and color depth of an image, the timing of an audio recording. They are defined per format and per collection, agreed with the designated community, and written into migration plans as acceptance criteria. Without named significant properties, validation has nothing to check and migrations preserve whatever the tool happened to keep. Naming them is a curatorial act: it declares what the content IS, as opposed to what its container happens to hold.",
 ["The characteristics that must survive any preservation action, named explicitly",
  "Defined per format and collection, agreed with the designated community",
  "Written into migration plans as validation acceptance criteria",
  "Without them, validation checks nothing and tools preserve accidents",
  "Naming significant properties declares what the content IS"],
 "Significant-property definitions per format, agreed with the designated community.",
 "ap_migrate"),

("Advanced",
 "Validation After Migration: Proving Fidelity",
 "Migration without validation is format roulette. Validation proves the significant properties survived: automated checks (byte-level comparison of text layers, page counts, image dimensions, duration), rendering spot-checks (humans viewing samples in the target format), and statistical sampling plans that balance confidence against effort. Failures are quarantined with logs, root-caused, and either re-migrated with fixed tooling or documented as accepted losses with curatorial sign-off. The validation report, with its pass rates and failure analysis, becomes part of the AIP's PREMIS events. Trust in migrated collections comes from validation reports, not from the migration tool's marketing.",
 ["Automated checks: text layers, counts, dimensions, durations compared",
  "Human rendering spot-checks on statistical samples",
  "Quarantine failures with logs; root-cause before re-migration",
  "Accepted losses need curatorial sign-off, never silent dropping",
  "The validation report joins the AIP as PREMIS events"],
 "Validation tooling with acceptance criteria and sampling plans, plus failure quarantine workflow.",
 "ap_migrate"),

("Advanced",
 "Checksum Manifests at Scale",
 "A thousand files need a manifest; a billion files need manifest infrastructure. At scale, manifests shard by collection, verify in parallel across workers, store in databases rather than flat files, and report through dashboards showing verification coverage and failure rates. Manifest formats stay simple (path, checksum, size) because simplicity survives tooling changes. Incremental verification - checking changed and due files rather than everything every time - keeps the load bounded. The manifest database itself is preserved: checksummed, replicated, backed by the same fixity regime as the content. At scale, the manifest system is a production service with SLAs, not a script someone runs.",
 ["Shard manifests by collection; verify in parallel; store in databases",
  "Keep manifest formats simple: path, checksum, size survives tooling churn",
  "Incremental verification bounds the load: check due and changed files",
  "Preserve the manifest database with the same fixity regime as content",
  "At scale, manifests are a production service with SLAs"],
 "A manifest database with parallel verification workers and coverage dashboards.",
 "ap_schedule"),

("Advanced",
 "Recovery Procedures: The Full Drill",
 "The recovery procedure is the disaster plan made executable: step-by-step instructions that a competent stranger can follow at 3 AM - declare the incident, assess affected collections from manifests, locate the newest verified replicas, restore to staging, verify checksums before returning to service, spot-check rendering, resume operations, and write the after-action report. Every step has a verification gate; restores are never trusted until checksums match. Drills run on schedule against staging environments, timed and scored, with failures fixed in the procedure, not excused. An untested recovery procedure is a rumor of a plan; a drilled one is a capability.",
 ["3 AM-readable steps: declare, assess, locate, restore, verify, resume, report",
  "Verification gates at every step; checksums must match before service resumes",
  "Drills on schedule in staging: timed, scored, procedure fixed on failure",
  "Never trust a restore until the manifest agrees",
  "Untested procedures are rumors; drilled procedures are capabilities"],
 "A written step-by-step recovery procedure with gates, rehearsed on schedule in staging.",
 "ap_recovery"),

("Advanced",
 "Forensic Recovery: Reading Dead Media",
 "When replicas are gone and the only copy sits on dead media, forensics is the last resort: specialized hardware reads failing drives bit by bit, imaging tools retry bad sectors, and file carvers reconstruct content from raw byte patterns without filesystem help. Success rates vary wildly - a scratched optical disc may yield 90 percent, a fire-damaged drive may yield nothing - and every attempt risks further damage, so write-blockers and bit-level imaging come before any repair attempt. Forensic recovery is expensive, slow, and uncertain, which is exactly why redundancy exists: forensics is the price of failed preservation planning. Document every attempt; even failures teach the next recovery.",
 ["Last resort when no healthy replica exists: bit-level imaging of failing media",
  "Write-blockers and imaging before any repair attempt - attempts can destroy",
  "File carvers rebuild content from byte patterns without filesystem help",
  "Expensive, slow, uncertain: forensics is the price of failed planning",
  "Document every attempt; failures teach the next recovery"],
 "Write-blockers, imaging hardware, and forensic tooling - plus the redundancy that should prevent needing them.",
 "ap_recovery"),

("Advanced",
 "Cryptographic Timestamping: Proving When",
 "Checksums prove what; timestamps prove when. Cryptographic timestamping (per RFC 3161) binds a file's hash to a trusted time source's signature, proving the content existed at that moment - defeating backdated forgeries and establishing priority. Trusted timestamp authorities or blockchain-anchored proofs provide the external anchor; the archive cannot timestamp itself credibly. Timestamps matter for legal admissibility, patent priority, and authenticity disputes decades later. Like checksums, timestamps are recorded as PREMIS events and preserved with the content. An archive that can prove both what and when holds evidence; one that proves only what holds assertions.",
 ["Bind content hashes to trusted time: proves existence at a moment",
  "Defeats backdating; establishes priority for legal and patent purposes",
  "External anchors required - self-timestamping is not credible",
  "RFC 3161 authorities or blockchain-anchored proofs",
  "What plus when equals evidence; what alone equals assertion"],
 "Access to a trusted timestamp authority and timestamp recording in PREMIS events.",
 "ap_checksum"),

("Advanced",
 "Preservation Cost Modeling",
 "Preservation is forever, so its costs must be modeled, not wished. Cost models account for storage (with redundancy multipliers and media refresh cycles), fixity compute and audit labor, migration projects (tooling, validation, staff time per format), metadata management, and administration - projected over decades with technology cost curves. Models reveal the real trade-offs: normalization reduces future migration costs; aggressive redundancy raises storage costs but prevents catastrophic loss; doing nothing is the most expensive option, priced in eventual total loss. Funders get honest numbers: the cost of keeping the promise. An archive without a cost model is a promise without a budget.",
 ["Model storage, fixity, migrations, metadata, administration over decades",
  "Include redundancy multipliers and media refresh cycles",
  "Normalization now reduces migration costs later - model the trade-off",
  "Doing nothing prices in eventual total loss: the most expensive option",
  "Honest numbers for funders: the cost of keeping the promise"],
 "A cost model with technology curves, refresh cycles, and migration project estimates.",
 "ap_cost"),

("Advanced",
 "Auditing Archives: TRAC and ISO 16363",
 "Trustworthy is a claim; audited is a fact. TRAC (Trustworthy Repositories Audit and Certification, from OCLC/RLG) and its successor ISO 16363 define the criteria: organizational viability, financial sustainability, technical infrastructure, and security - over a hundred checkable requirements. Audits examine documentation, observe practices, and test systems: can the archive prove fixity, demonstrate migrations, show succession plans? Certification is public, which lets depositors and funders distinguish real archives from storage with marketing. Self-audit against the criteria first, remediate gaps, then invite external auditors. The audit is not the enemy of the archive; it is how the archive proves it deserves its collections.",
 ["TRAC/ISO 16363: 100+ checkable criteria for trustworthy repositories",
  "Covers organization, finance, technology, security - not just bits",
  "Auditors test: prove fixity, demonstrate migrations, show succession",
  "Certification is public; it separates archives from marketing",
  "Self-audit first, remediate, then invite external auditors"],
 "The ISO 16363 criteria as a self-audit checklist with remediation tracking.",
 "ap_schedule"),

("Advanced",
 "The 100-Year Archive: Designing for Deep Time",
 "Designing for a century forces honesty about every assumption: storage media will be replaced many times, formats will die repeatedly, institutions will merge and split, staff will turn over completely, and today's software will be archaeology. The 100-year archive therefore designs for change itself - media-independent packaging, format-agnostic metadata, self-describing AIPs carrying their own representation information, succession plans with named heirs, and endowments structured for perpetual care. It keeps paper fallbacks for the most critical documentation, because paper's failure modes are understood over centuries. The century archive is not a bigger hard drive; it is an institution engineered to outlive its engineers.",
 ["Assume media, formats, institutions, and staff all turn over completely",
  "Design for change: media-independent packages, self-describing AIPs",
  "Succession with named heirs; endowments for perpetual care",
  "Paper fallbacks for critical docs - centuries of understood failure modes",
  "A century archive is an institution, not a bigger hard drive"],
 "Century-horizon planning documents, endowed funding, and named institutional successors.",
 "ap_cost"),
]
